// Percentages bank 2: growth-depreciation, elections, income-savings, exam-marks, back-calc (original questions)
QB.add([
  {"id":"pct-grow-01","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"growth-depreciation","d":"Easy","q":"The population of a town is **80,000** and it grows by **5%** every year. What will it be after **2 years**?","opts":["88,200","88,000","84,000","92,610"],"ans":0,"time":35,"sol":["Growth factor is 1.05 per year","80,000 × 1.05 × 1.05 = 80,000 × 1.1025 = **88,200**"],"short":"Two years at 5% = 5 + 5 + 0.25 = 10.25%, so 80,000 + 8,200.","trap":"Doubling the rate to 10% ignores growth on last year's growth.","why":[null,"Used simple growth: 10% of 80,000","Stopped after one year","Compounded for 3 years"],"calc":"80000*1.05*1.05"},
  {"id":"pct-grow-02","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"growth-depreciation","d":"Easy","q":"A machine worth **₹50,000** loses **10%** of its value every year. What is it worth after **2 years**?","opts":["₹40,000","₹40,500","₹45,000","₹60,500"],"ans":1,"time":35,"sol":["Value after year 1 = 50,000 × 0.9 = 45,000","Value after year 2 = 45,000 × 0.9 = **₹40,500**"],"short":"Two falls of 10% = −10 −10 + 1 = −19%, so 81% of 50,000.","trap":"A flat 20% cut forgets that year 2's 10% is taken on a smaller value.","why":["Took a flat 20% off the original",null,"Applied only one year of depreciation","Compounded upward instead of down"],"calc":"50000*0.9*0.9"},
  {"id":"pct-grow-03","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"growth-depreciation","d":"Easy","q":"A scooter bought for **₹60,000** loses **15%** of its value in the first year. What is it worth at the end of that year?","opts":["₹9,000","₹69,000","₹51,000","≈ ₹52,174"],"ans":2,"time":30,"sol":["Loss = 15% of 60,000 = 9,000","Value = 60,000 − 9,000 = **₹51,000**"],"short":"Multiply by 0.85 directly: 60,000 × 0.85.","trap":"Read the question again: it asks for the value left, not the loss.","why":["Gave the loss, not the remaining value","Added 15% instead of subtracting",null,"Divided by 1.15 instead of multiplying by 0.85"],"calc":"60000*0.85"},
  {"id":"pct-grow-04","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"growth-depreciation","d":"Easy","q":"The population of a village is now **1,32,000**, after growing **10%** over last year. What was it a year ago?","opts":["1,18,800","1,45,200","1,22,000","1,20,000"],"ans":3,"time":40,"sol":["Today = last year × 1.10","Last year = 1,32,000 ÷ 1.1 = **1,20,000**"],"short":"Divide by 1.1: 132 ÷ 1.1 = 120.","trap":"The 10% is of last year's value, not of today's, so never subtract 10% of the new figure.","why":["Subtracted 10% of today's figure","Added 10% instead of removing it","Subtracted a flat 10,000",null],"calc":"132000/1.1"},
  {"id":"pct-grow-05","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"growth-depreciation","d":"Easy","q":"A town of **40,000** people sees its population fall by **5%** every year. How many people live there after **2 years**?","opts":["36,000","36,100","38,000","44,100"],"ans":1,"time":40,"sol":["After year 1: 40,000 × 0.95 = 38,000","After year 2: 38,000 × 0.95 = **36,100**"],"short":"−5 −5 + 0.25 = −9.75%, so 40,000 − 3,900.","trap":"Two 5% falls are not a 10% fall; the second 5% is on 38,000.","why":["Took a flat 10% off",null,"Applied only one year","Grew by 5% instead of falling"],"calc":"40000*0.95*0.95"},
  {"id":"pct-grow-06","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"growth-depreciation","d":"Medium","q":"The population of a city is **2,50,000**. It grows by **10%** in the first year and by **20%** in the second year. What is it after two years?","opts":["3,25,000","3,30,625","3,30,000","3,60,000"],"ans":2,"time":60,"sol":["Multiply the factors: 1.10 × 1.20 = 1.32","2,50,000 × 1.32 = **3,30,000**"],"short":"10 + 20 + (10×20)/100 = 32%, so 2,50,000 × 1.32.","trap":"Adding 10% + 20% = 30% misses the 2% growth on growth.","why":["Added 10% + 20% = 30% on the original","Used the average 15% for both years",null,"Used 20% for both years"],"calc":"250000*1.1*1.2"},
  {"id":"pct-grow-07","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"growth-depreciation","d":"Medium","q":"A car costing **₹8,00,000** loses **20%** of its value in the first year and **15%** of its remaining value in the second year. What is its value after two years?","opts":["₹5,20,000","₹5,44,500","₹6,80,000","₹5,44,000"],"ans":3,"time":65,"sol":["After year 1: 8,00,000 × 0.80 = 6,40,000","After year 2: 6,40,000 × 0.85 = **₹5,44,000**"],"short":"Net = −20 −15 + (20×15)/100 = −32%, so 68% of 8,00,000.","trap":"Successive falls are never just added: the second one acts on a smaller base.","why":["Took off 20% + 15% = 35% in one go","Used the average 17.5% for both years","Applied only the 15% rate",null],"calc":"800000*0.8*0.85"},
  {"id":"pct-grow-08","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"growth-depreciation","d":"Medium","q":"The population of a town is now **9,261**. It has grown by **5%** every year for the last **3 years**. What was it 3 years ago?","opts":["8,000","8,820","≈ 8,053","≈ 7,872"],"ans":0,"time":70,"sol":["Three years of 5% growth: 1.05³ = 1.157625","9,261 ÷ 1.157625 = **8,000**"],"short":"9,261 = 21³ and 1.05 = 21/20, so the start is 20³ = 8,000.","trap":"Undoing three 5% rises is not one 15% cut.","why":[null,"Undid only one year of growth","Divided by 1.15 (three 5% rises added)","Took 15% off today's figure"],"calc":"9261/Math.pow(1.05,3)"},
  {"id":"pct-grow-09","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"growth-depreciation","d":"Medium","q":"A machine bought for **₹2,40,000** depreciates by **12.5%** of its current value every year. What is its value after **2 years**?","opts":["₹1,80,000","₹2,10,000","₹1,83,750","₹3,03,750"],"ans":2,"time":60,"sol":["12.5% = 1/8, so the factor is 7/8 per year","2,40,000 × 7/8 × 7/8 = **₹1,83,750**"],"short":"Use fractions: 2,40,000 → 2,10,000 → 1,83,750.","trap":"The second 12.5% is of the reduced value, so the total fall is less than 25%.","why":["Took a flat 25% off the original","Applied only one year",null,"Grew by 12.5% twice instead of falling"],"calc":"240000*7/8*7/8"},
  {"id":"pct-grow-10","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"growth-depreciation","d":"Medium","q":"The population of a town is **1,60,000**. It rises by **20%** in the first year and falls by **10%** in the second year. What is it after two years?","opts":["1,76,000","1,92,000","1,44,000","1,72,800"],"ans":3,"time":65,"sol":["Factors: 1.20 and 0.90, product 1.08","1,60,000 × 1.08 = **1,72,800**"],"short":"+20 −10 −2 = +8%, so 1,60,000 + 12,800.","trap":"A rise and a fall of different sizes do not simply subtract.","why":["Netted 20% − 10% = +10% on the original","Ignored the fall in year two","Ignored the rise in year one",null],"calc":"160000*1.2*0.9"},
  {"id":"pct-grow-11","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"growth-depreciation","d":"Medium","q":"A town had **50,000** people. Its population rose **20%** in 2021, rose **10%** in 2022 and then fell **10%** in 2023. How many people are there at the end of 2023?","opts":["59,400","60,000","66,000","45,000"],"ans":0,"time":70,"sol":["50,000 × 1.2 = 60,000; × 1.1 = 66,000","66,000 × 0.9 = **59,400**"],"short":"1.2 × 1.1 = 1.32, then 1.32 × 0.9 = 1.188, so 59,400.","trap":"Rises and falls in a chain must be multiplied, not netted.","why":[null,"Added 20 + 10 − 10 = +20% flat","Left out the 2023 fall","Left out both rises"],"calc":"50000*1.2*1.1*0.9"},
  {"id":"pct-grow-12","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"growth-depreciation","d":"Hard","q":"A town's population rose **20%** in one year and then fell **10%** the next year. It now stands at **2,16,000**. What was it at the start of the first year?","opts":["2,40,000","2,00,000","1,80,000","2,33,280"],"ans":1,"time":90,"sol":["Net factor = 1.20 × 0.90 = 1.08","Start = 2,16,000 ÷ 1.08 = **2,00,000**"],"short":"Net change is +8% (20 − 10 − 2), so divide by 1.08.","trap":"Undoing a chain means dividing by the net factor, not reversing just one step.","why":["Undid only the 10% fall",null,"Undid only the 20% rise","Multiplied by 1.08 instead of dividing"],"calc":"216000/(1.2*0.9)"},
  {"id":"pct-grow-13","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"growth-depreciation","d":"Hard","q":"A car costing **₹10,00,000** is worth **₹6,40,000** after **2 years**. If it lost the same percentage of its value each year, what was the yearly rate?","opts":["18%","36%","80%","20%"],"ans":3,"time":80,"sol":["Remaining fraction after 2 years = 6.4 ÷ 10 = 0.64","Yearly factor = √0.64 = 0.8, so the rate is **20%**"],"short":"0.64 = 0.8², so the yearly fall is 100 − 80 = 20%.","trap":"Splitting 36% into two equal parts ignores compounding.","why":["Halved the 36% total fall","Gave the total fall, not the yearly rate","Gave the remaining fraction per year",null],"calc":"(1-Math.sqrt(640000/1000000))*100"},
  {"id":"pct-grow-14","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"growth-depreciation","d":"Hard","q":"The population of a town grows **10%** every year. After **3 years** it is **66,550**. By how much did it increase during the third year alone?","opts":["6,050","6,655","16,550","5,500"],"ans":0,"time":100,"sol":["End of year 2 = 66,550 ÷ 1.1 = 60,500","Third-year increase = 66,550 − 60,500 = **6,050**"],"short":"Last year's rise is the final value × 10/110, i.e. 66,550 ÷ 11 = 6,050.","trap":"10% of the final figure is more than the real rise, because the rise is on the year-start value.","why":[null,"Took 10% of the final figure","Gave the total rise over 3 years","Gave the second-year increase"],"calc":"66550-66550/1.1"},
  {"id":"pct-grow-15","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"growth-depreciation","d":"Hard","q":"A machine loses **20%** of its current value every year. After how many whole years does its value first drop below **half** of its original value?","opts":["3 years","4 years","2 years","5 years"],"ans":1,"time":100,"sol":["Remaining fractions: 0.8, 0.64, 0.512, 0.4096","0.512 is still above 0.5; 0.4096 is below it, so **4 years**"],"short":"Write 0.8, 0.64, 0.512, 0.41 and stop at the first value under 0.5.","trap":"Thinking 20% of the original goes each year gives 2.5 years; depreciation shrinks each year.","why":["Read 0.512 as already below half",null,"Linear: 50 ÷ 20 = 2.5, rounded down","Off by one: counted one extra year"],"calc":"4"},
  {"id":"pct-grow-16","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"growth-depreciation","d":"Hard","q":"The population of a town increased by **20%** in 2019, by **30%** in 2020 and decreased by **10%** in 2021. What is the net percentage change over the three years?","opts":["40%","50%","40.4%","46%"],"ans":2,"time":90,"sol":["Multiply: 1.20 × 1.30 × 0.90 = 1.404","Net change = **+40.4%**"],"short":"1.2 × 1.3 = 1.56, then × 0.9 = 1.404.","trap":"Adding the three percentages (40%) looks right but misses the compounding.","why":["Added 20 + 30 − 10 directly","Ignored the fall and added 20 + 30",null,"Subtracted 10 points from the 156% level"],"calc":"(1.2*1.3*0.9-1)*100"},
  {"id":"pct-elect-01","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"elections","d":"Easy","q":"In a two-candidate contest, the winner got **60%** of the **50,000** valid votes. By how many votes did the winner win?","opts":["10,000","30,000","20,000","5,000"],"ans":0,"time":35,"sol":["Loser got 100 − 60 = 40%","Margin = (60 − 40)% of 50,000 = 20% × 50,000 = **10,000**"],"short":"Margin % = winner % − loser % = 2 × 60 − 100 = 20%.","trap":"The margin is the difference of the two, not either candidate's votes.","why":[null,"Gave the winner's votes","Gave the loser's votes","Halved the margin"],"calc":"50000*0.2"},
  {"id":"pct-elect-02","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"elections","d":"Easy","q":"In an election between two candidates, one candidate got **45%** of the votes and lost by **2,000** votes. How many votes were polled in total?","opts":["≈ 4,444","20,000","40,000","10,000"],"ans":1,"time":40,"sol":["Winner got 55%, so margin = 55 − 45 = 10% of total","Total = 2,000 ÷ 0.10 = **20,000**"],"short":"Margin = 10% of total, so total = margin × 10.","trap":"The margin is 10% of the total, not 55% − 45% = 10 of one candidate's share.","why":["Divided the margin by 45% only",null,"Divided the margin by 5%, not 10%","Treated the margin as 20% of the total"],"calc":"2000/0.1"},
  {"id":"pct-elect-03","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"elections","d":"Easy","q":"Of **1,00,000** registered voters, **20%** did not vote. The winner of the two-way race got **50%** of the votes cast. How many votes did the winner get?","opts":["50,000","80,000","40,000","10,000"],"ans":2,"time":40,"sol":["Votes cast = 80% of 1,00,000 = 80,000","Winner = 50% of 80,000 = **40,000**"],"short":"Half of 80% of 1,00,000 = 40% of 1,00,000.","trap":"The percentage applies to votes cast, not to registered voters.","why":["Took 50% of all registered voters","Gave the number who actually voted",null,"Took 50% of the non-voters"],"calc":"100000*0.8*0.5"},
  {"id":"pct-elect-04","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"elections","d":"Easy","q":"In an election **1,00,000** votes were polled and **10%** of them were invalid. The winner of the two-candidate race got **55%** of the valid votes. How many votes did the winner get?","opts":["55,000","40,500","45,000","49,500"],"ans":3,"time":45,"sol":["Valid votes = 90% of 1,00,000 = 90,000","Winner = 55% of 90,000 = **49,500**"],"short":"55% of 90,000 = 50% (45,000) + 5% (4,500).","trap":"Percentages of the winner refer to valid votes only.","why":["Took 55% of all polled votes","Gave the loser's votes","Took half of the valid votes",null],"calc":"100000*0.9*0.55"},
  {"id":"pct-elect-05","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"elections","d":"Easy","q":"A candidate won an election by a margin of **12%** of the total valid votes, which was **2,400** votes. How many valid votes were there?","opts":["28,800","20,000","2,00,000","≈ 2,727"],"ans":1,"time":45,"sol":["12% of valid votes = 2,400","Valid votes = 2,400 ÷ 0.12 = **20,000**"],"short":"1% = 2,400 ÷ 12 = 200, so 100% = 20,000.","trap":"A percentage like 12% is 0.12, not 0.012.","why":["Multiplied the margin by 12",null,"Divided by 1.2% instead of 12%","Divided by 88%"],"calc":"2400/0.12"},
  {"id":"pct-elect-06","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"elections","d":"Medium","q":"In a three-way contest A, B and C got **45%**, **35%** and **20%** of the valid votes. **1,20,000** votes were polled and **5%** were invalid. By how many votes did A beat C?","opts":["30,000","22,800","28,500","51,300"],"ans":2,"time":70,"sol":["Valid votes = 95% of 1,20,000 = 1,14,000","A − C = (45 − 20)% of 1,14,000 = **28,500**"],"short":"25% of 1,14,000 = 28,500.","trap":"Remove invalid votes before taking percentages of the valid ones.","why":["Used all 1,20,000 polled votes","Gave C's votes only",null,"Gave A's votes only"],"calc":"120000*0.95*0.25"},
  {"id":"pct-elect-07","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"elections","d":"Medium","q":"Of **80,000** registered voters, **25%** did not vote. Of the votes cast, **4%** were invalid. The winner of the two-way race got **55%** of the valid votes. What was the winning margin?","opts":["6,000","8,000","11,520","5,760"],"ans":3,"time":75,"sol":["Cast = 75% of 80,000 = 60,000; valid = 96% of that = 57,600","Margin = (55 − 45)% of 57,600 = **5,760**"],"short":"Margin = 10% of valid votes = 10% × 57,600.","trap":"Work down the chain: registered, then cast, then valid.","why":["Forgot the invalid votes","Forgot both the non-voters and invalid votes","Took 20% instead of 10%",null],"calc":"80000*0.75*0.96*0.10"},
  {"id":"pct-elect-08","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"elections","d":"Medium","q":"In a two-candidate election, **8%** of the votes polled were invalid. The winner got **52%** of the valid votes and won by **3,680** votes. How many votes were polled?","opts":["1,00,000","92,000","99,360","84,640"],"ans":0,"time":70,"sol":["Margin = (52 − 48)% = 4% of valid votes = 3,680, so valid = 92,000","Polled = 92,000 ÷ 0.92 = **1,00,000**"],"short":"4% = 3,680 → valid = 92,000 = 92% of polled → polled = 1,00,000.","trap":"Valid votes are 92% of polled, so divide by 0.92 (do not add 8%).","why":[null,"Gave the valid votes, not the polled votes","Added 8% to the valid votes","Took 92% of the valid votes again"],"calc":"3680/0.04/0.92"},
  {"id":"pct-elect-09","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"elections","d":"Medium","q":"In a three-candidate election A got **8,400** votes, which is **35%** of the votes polled. B got **28%** of the votes polled. How many votes did C get?","opts":["6,720","9,600","8,880","15,120"],"ans":2,"time":60,"sol":["Total = 8,400 ÷ 0.35 = 24,000","C's share = 100 − 35 − 28 = 37%, so C = 0.37 × 24,000 = **8,880**"],"short":"1% = 240 votes, so C = 37 × 240.","trap":"Subtract carefully: 100 − 35 − 28 = 37, not 40.","why":["Gave B's votes","Slipped: used 40% for C instead of 37%",null,"Gave A + B together"],"calc":"8400/0.35*0.37"},
  {"id":"pct-elect-10","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"elections","d":"Medium","q":"In a ward, **30%** of registered voters did not vote, and **5%** of those who voted cast invalid ballots. The winner of a two-candidate race got **52%** of the valid votes and won by **1,596** votes. How many voters were registered?","opts":["42,000","39,900","57,000","60,000"],"ans":3,"time":75,"sol":["Margin = 4% of valid = 1,596, so valid = 39,900","Cast = 39,900 ÷ 0.95 = 42,000; registered = 42,000 ÷ 0.70 = **60,000**"],"short":"Work backwards one filter at a time: ÷4%, ÷95%, ÷70%.","trap":"Each filter (invalid, absent) must be undone separately.","why":["Gave the votes cast, not the registered voters","Gave the valid votes, not the registered voters","Skipped the invalid-vote step",null],"calc":"1596/0.04/0.95/0.7"},
  {"id":"pct-elect-11","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"elections","d":"Medium","q":"In a three-way election P, Q and R got **38%**, **34%** and **28%** of the **50,000** valid votes. By how many votes did the winner beat the runner-up?","opts":["2,000","5,000","19,000","1,000"],"ans":0,"time":60,"sol":["Winner P has 38%, runner-up Q has 34%","Gap = 4% of 50,000 = **2,000**"],"short":"4% of 50,000 = 2,000.","trap":"Beat the runner-up means the second place, not the last.","why":[null,"Compared the winner with the last candidate","Gave the winner's votes","Halved the gap"],"calc":"50000*0.04"},
  {"id":"pct-elect-12","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"elections","d":"Hard","q":"In a two-candidate election **20%** of registered voters did not vote and **5%** of the votes cast were invalid. The winner got **55%** of the valid votes and won by **1,520** votes. How many voters were registered?","opts":["16,000","20,000","15,200","19,000"],"ans":1,"time":100,"sol":["Margin = 10% of valid = 1,520, so valid = 15,200","Cast = 15,200 ÷ 0.95 = 16,000; registered = 16,000 ÷ 0.8 = **20,000**"],"short":"Chain backwards: ×10, ÷0.95, ÷0.8.","trap":"Two separate filters, so two separate divisions.","why":["Gave the votes cast",null,"Gave the valid votes","Skipped the invalid-vote step"],"calc":"1520/0.1/0.95/0.8"},
  {"id":"pct-elect-13","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"elections","d":"Hard","q":"In a two-candidate race A leads B by **10%** of the **40,000** votes polled. If **1,000** voters switch from A to B, who wins and by how much?","opts":["A by 3,000","A by 4,000","A by 6,000","A by 2,000"],"ans":3,"time":90,"sol":["Original lead = 10% of 40,000 = 4,000","Each switch cuts A's lead by 2 votes (A −1, B +1): 4,000 − 2,000 = **A by 2,000**"],"short":"A switch moves a vote from one side to the other, so the lead changes by twice the number.","trap":"A switched vote counts double on the margin.","why":["Counted the switch as a 1,000 swing","Ignored the switch","Added the swing instead of subtracting",null],"calc":"4000-2*1000"},
  {"id":"pct-elect-14","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"elections","d":"Hard","q":"In a three-way election the valid votes number **90,000**. Candidate A got **50% more** votes than B, and B got **20% fewer** votes than C. What was the winning margin (winner minus runner-up)?","opts":["6,000","12,000","36,000","7,200"],"ans":0,"time":100,"sol":["Let C = 100k: B = 80k, A = 1.5 × 80k = 120k; total 300k = 90,000, so k = 300","A = 36,000, C = 30,000, B = 24,000; margin A − C = **6,000**"],"short":"Ratio A : B : C = 120 : 80 : 100 (total 300), so margin = 20 parts = 20/300 of 90,000.","trap":"The runner-up is C here, not B.","why":[null,"Gave A minus B","Gave the winner's votes","Took 20% of A's votes"],"calc":"90000*20/300"},
  {"id":"pct-elect-15","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"elections","d":"Hard","q":"Women are **40%** of the voters and men **60%**. The winner got **65%** of the women's votes and **45%** of the men's votes. What percentage of all votes did the winner get?","opts":["55%","53%","57%","26%"],"ans":1,"time":90,"sol":["Women's part = 0.40 × 65 = 26","Men's part = 0.60 × 45 = 27; total = **53%**"],"short":"Weighted average: 45 + 0.4 × (65 − 45) = 53.","trap":"A simple average ignores that men are more numerous.","why":["Took the simple average of 65 and 45",null,"Swapped the weights","Counted only the women's part"],"calc":"0.4*65+0.6*45"},
  {"id":"pct-elect-16","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"elections","d":"Hard","q":"In a two-candidate election **75%** of the registered voters voted. The winner got **60%** of the votes cast and won by **4,500** votes. How many voters were registered?","opts":["22,500","10,000","30,000","16,875"],"ans":2,"time":100,"sol":["Margin = (60 − 40)% = 20% of votes cast = 4,500, so cast = 22,500","Registered = 22,500 ÷ 0.75 = **30,000**"],"short":"Margin = 20% of cast; cast = 75% of registered, so 4,500 = 15% of registered.","trap":"Cast votes are a part of registered voters, so divide by 0.75 to go back.","why":["Gave the votes cast","Took 4,500 as the winner's 45% of registered",null,"Multiplied by 75% instead of dividing"],"calc":"4500/0.2/0.75"},
  {"id":"pct-save-01","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"income-savings","d":"Easy","q":"Meera earns **₹40,000** a month and spends **75%** of it. How much does she save?","opts":["₹10,000","₹30,000","₹7,500","₹25,000"],"ans":0,"time":30,"sol":["Savings = 100 − 75 = 25% of income","25% of 40,000 = **₹10,000**"],"short":"Savings are a quarter of income: 40,000 ÷ 4.","trap":"Spending 75% leaves 25%, not 75%, as savings.","why":[null,"Gave the expenditure","Took 75% of ₹10,000","Read 25% as ₹25,000"],"calc":"40000*0.25"},
  {"id":"pct-save-02","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"income-savings","d":"Easy","q":"Ravi saves **₹6,000** a month, which is **20%** of his income. What is his income?","opts":["₹1,200","₹30,000","₹7,200","₹24,000"],"ans":1,"time":30,"sol":["20% of income = 6,000","Income = 6,000 ÷ 0.20 = **₹30,000**"],"short":"20% = 1/5, so income = 5 × savings.","trap":"The 20% is of income, so divide; do not apply it to the savings.","why":["Took 20% of the savings",null,"Added 20% to the savings","Gave the expenditure"],"calc":"6000/0.2"},
  {"id":"pct-save-03","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"income-savings","d":"Easy","q":"A family earns **₹50,000** a month and spends **₹38,000**. What percentage of income do they save?","opts":["76%","≈ 31.6%","24%","12%"],"ans":2,"time":35,"sol":["Savings = 50,000 − 38,000 = 12,000","Savings % = 12,000 ÷ 50,000 × 100 = **24%**"],"short":"Savings 12 of 50 = 24 of 100.","trap":"Percent of income, so the denominator is income, not spending.","why":["Gave the expenditure percentage","Divided savings by expenditure",null,"Wrote ₹12,000 as 12%"],"calc":"12000/50000*100"},
  {"id":"pct-save-04","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"income-savings","d":"Easy","q":"Anil earns **₹20,000** and saves **₹4,000**. His income rises by **10%** and his spending stays the same. What are his new savings?","opts":["₹4,400","₹22,000","₹4,000","₹6,000"],"ans":3,"time":40,"sol":["Spending = 20,000 − 4,000 = 16,000; new income = 22,000","New savings = 22,000 − 16,000 = **₹6,000**"],"short":"Extra income goes wholly into savings: 4,000 + 2,000.","trap":"Savings rise by the full extra income, not by 10% of savings.","why":["Raised savings by 10%","Gave the new income","Assumed savings do not change",null],"calc":"20000*1.1-16000"},
  {"id":"pct-save-05","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"income-savings","d":"Easy","q":"Kiran spends **3/5** of her income on household, **1/10** on travel, and saves **₹9,000**. What is her income?","opts":["₹22,500","₹30,000","₹90,000","≈ ₹12,857"],"ans":1,"time":45,"sol":["Savings share = 1 − 3/5 − 1/10 = 3/10","Income = 9,000 ÷ (3/10) = **₹30,000**"],"short":"Savings are 3 parts out of 10, so one part = 3,000.","trap":"Subtract every spending share, not just the first.","why":["Forgot the travel share",null,"Treated savings as 1/10 of income","Divided by 70%, the spending share"],"calc":"9000/0.3"},
  {"id":"pct-save-06","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"income-savings","d":"Medium","q":"Neeraj has a salary of **₹36,000**. He spends 40% on rent, 25% on food and 15% on other items, and saves the rest. How much does he save?","opts":["₹28,800","₹9,000","₹7,200","₹5,400"],"ans":2,"time":60,"sol":["Spending = 40 + 25 + 15 = 80%","Savings = 20% of 36,000 = **₹7,200**"],"short":"Savings = 20% = 1/5 of 36,000.","trap":"Add all the spending shares before subtracting from 100.","why":["Gave the total spending","Took the 25% food share as savings",null,"Took the 15% share as savings"],"calc":"36000*0.2"},
  {"id":"pct-save-07","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"income-savings","d":"Medium","q":"Sneha earns **₹50,000** and spends **₹40,000**. Next year her income rises **20%** and her spending rises **10%**. What are her new savings?","opts":["₹11,000","₹20,000","₹6,000","₹16,000"],"ans":3,"time":65,"sol":["New income = 60,000; new spending = 44,000","New savings = 60,000 − 44,000 = **₹16,000**"],"short":"Change in savings = +10,000 − 4,000 = +6,000 on top of 10,000.","trap":"Savings is a difference, so never apply a single % to it.","why":["Raised the old savings by 10%","Kept spending unchanged","Subtracted new spending from the old income",null],"calc":"50000*1.2-40000*1.1"},
  {"id":"pct-save-08","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"income-savings","d":"Medium","q":"Rohit earns **₹60,000** and spends **80%** of it. His income rises **25%** and his spending rises **10%**. What percentage of his new income does he save?","opts":["29.6%","20%","15%","22.2%"],"ans":0,"time":75,"sol":["Spending = 48,000 → 52,800; income = 75,000","Savings = 22,200; rate = 22,200 ÷ 75,000 = **29.6%**"],"short":"Take income 100, spending 80: new 125 and 88, savings 37 of 125 = 29.6%.","trap":"The saving rate changes when income and spending change by different amounts.","why":[null,"Assumed the saving rate is unchanged","Subtracted 10 from 25","Read ₹22,200 as 22.2%"],"calc":"(60000*1.25-60000*0.8*1.1)/(60000*1.25)*100"},
  {"id":"pct-save-09","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"income-savings","d":"Medium","q":"Vikram saves **₹8,000**, which is **1/5** of his income. His spending rises by **20%** while his income stays the same. What are his new savings?","opts":["₹6,400","₹38,400","₹1,600","₹9,600"],"ans":2,"time":70,"sol":["Income = 8,000 × 5 = 40,000; spending = 32,000","New spending = 38,400, so savings = 40,000 − 38,400 = **₹1,600**"],"short":"Spending is 4/5 of income; 20% more of that is 6,400, which cuts 8,000 to 1,600.","trap":"A rise in spending reduces savings by the same amount.","why":["Gave the extra spending","Gave the new spending",null,"Added the extra instead of subtracting"],"calc":"40000-32000*1.2"},
  {"id":"pct-save-10","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"income-savings","d":"Medium","q":"Anita saves **15%** of her income. Her expenses are **₹34,000** a month. What is her income?","opts":["₹39,100","₹28,900","₹5,100","₹40,000"],"ans":3,"time":55,"sol":["Expenses = 85% of income","Income = 34,000 ÷ 0.85 = **₹40,000**"],"short":"85% = 34,000, so 5% = 2,000 and 100% = 40,000.","trap":"The 15% is of income, so divide expenses by 0.85.","why":["Added 15% to the expenses","Took 85% of the expenses","Gave 15% of the expenses",null],"calc":"34000/0.85"},
  {"id":"pct-save-11","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"income-savings","d":"Medium","q":"Ravi earns **₹45,000**. He spends **60%** on household, then **15% of the remaining** money on travel, and saves the rest. What are his savings?","opts":["₹15,300","₹18,000","₹11,250","₹2,700"],"ans":0,"time":70,"sol":["After household: 40% of 45,000 = 18,000","Travel = 15% of 18,000 = 2,700; savings = 18,000 − 2,700 = **₹15,300**"],"short":"Savings = 85% of 40% of 45,000 = 34% of 45,000.","trap":"Travel is 15% of what remains, not of the full income.","why":[null,"Forgot the travel spending","Took travel as 15% of total income","Gave the travel amount only"],"calc":"45000*0.4*0.85"},
  {"id":"pct-save-12","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"income-savings","d":"Hard","q":"A man's income rises **20%** and his expenditure rises **10%**, so his savings rise **50%**. What percentage of his original income did he save?","opts":["75%","25%","20%","40%"],"ans":1,"time":110,"sol":["Let income = 100, expenditure = x, savings = 100 − x","120 − 1.1x = 1.5(100 − x) → 0.4x = 30 → x = 75, so savings = **25%**"],"short":"Extra income 20 = extra spending 0.1x + extra savings 0.5(100 − x); solve for x = 75.","trap":"Define savings as income minus expenditure and set up one equation.","why":["Gave the expenditure share",null,"Copied the income rise","Gave the coefficient 0.4 from the equation"],"calc":"100-75"},
  {"id":"pct-save-13","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"income-savings","d":"Hard","q":"Neha saves **20%** of her income. Her income falls by **10%**. By what percentage must she cut her spending to keep her savings amount unchanged?","opts":["10%","87.5%","≈ 14.3%","12.5%"],"ans":3,"time":100,"sol":["Income 100, spending 80, savings 20; new income 90","Spending must be 70, so cut = 10 ÷ 80 = **12.5%**"],"short":"Cut in rupees = fall in income = 10, taken on the old spending of 80.","trap":"The cut is a percentage of the old spending.","why":["Matched the income fall","Gave the remaining fraction","Divided the cut by the new spending",null],"calc":"10/80*100"},
  {"id":"pct-save-14","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"income-savings","d":"Hard","q":"Arun's income is **25% more** than Varun's. Arun saves **20%** of his income and Varun saves **10%** of his. Arun's savings are what percentage more than Varun's?","opts":["150%","250%","100%","15%"],"ans":0,"time":100,"sol":["Varun's income 100, Arun's 125","Savings: Arun 25, Varun 10, so Arun is (25 − 10)/10 = **150%** more"],"short":"Savings ratio = 125 × 20 : 100 × 10 = 5 : 2, so 3/2 more = 150%.","trap":"More than is (difference ÷ Varun's), not the ratio itself.","why":[null,"Gave Arun's savings as a % of Varun's","Compared 20% with 10% directly","Subtracted 10 from 25"],"calc":"(125*0.2-100*0.1)/(100*0.1)*100"},
  {"id":"pct-save-15","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"income-savings","d":"Hard","q":"A family earns **₹80,000** a month. 30% goes to rent. Of the remainder, 25% goes on food. Of what is then left, one-third goes on education. The rest is saved. What percentage of income is saved?","opts":["45%","35%","28%","42%"],"ans":1,"time":100,"sol":["After rent 56,000; food 14,000 leaves 42,000","Education 14,000 leaves 28,000, which is 28,000 ÷ 80,000 = **35%**"],"short":"Keep fractions: 0.7 × 0.75 × (2/3) = 0.35.","trap":"Each deduction is on what remains, so the shares multiply.","why":["Subtracted 30 and 25 straight from 100",null,"Read ₹28,000 as a percentage","Stopped before education: ₹42,000"],"calc":"0.7*0.75*(2/3)*100"},
  {"id":"pct-save-16","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"income-savings","d":"Hard","q":"Sumit saves **12%** of his salary. His salary rises by **25%**, but he spends the same amount as before, so his new savings are **₹14,800**. What was his original salary?","opts":["≈ ₹1,23,333","₹1,18,400","₹40,000","₹11,840"],"ans":2,"time":110,"sol":["Spending = 88% of S; new income = 1.25 S","New savings = 1.25S − 0.88S = 0.37S = 14,800, so S = **₹40,000**"],"short":"New saving rate = 125 − 88 = 37% of the old salary.","trap":"Spending is fixed in rupees, so the new saving rate is not 12%.","why":["Divided the new savings by 12%","Divided by 12.5%",null,"Divided by 1.25 only"],"calc":"14800/0.37"},
  {"id":"pct-marks-01","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"exam-marks","d":"Easy","q":"The pass mark in an exam is **40%** of **600**. Ankit scores **210**. By how many marks does he fail?","opts":["30","240","390","60"],"ans":0,"time":35,"sol":["Pass mark = 40% of 600 = 240","Shortfall = 240 − 210 = **30**"],"short":"40% of 600 is 240; 240 − 210.","trap":"The question asks for the shortfall, not the pass mark itself.","why":[null,"Gave the pass mark","Gave 600 − 210","Doubled the gap"],"calc":"600*0.4-210"},
  {"id":"pct-marks-02","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"exam-marks","d":"Easy","q":"Pooja scored **396** out of **600**. What is her percentage?","opts":["34%","66%","99%","6.6%"],"ans":1,"time":30,"sol":["Percentage = 396 ÷ 600 × 100","= **66%**"],"short":"396 ÷ 6 = 66.","trap":"Divide by the maximum marks, not by a round figure.","why":["Gave the marks lost",null,"Divided by 400","Slipped a decimal place"],"calc":"396/600*100"},
  {"id":"pct-marks-03","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"exam-marks","d":"Easy","q":"The pass mark is **35%**. A student who got **98** marks failed by **7** marks. What are the maximum marks?","opts":["280","105","300","350"],"ans":2,"time":40,"sol":["Pass mark = 98 + 7 = 105","35% of max = 105, so max = 105 ÷ 0.35 = **300**"],"short":"105 ÷ 35 = 3, so 100% = 300.","trap":"Failing by 7 means pass mark = marks + 7.","why":["Ignored the failing margin","Gave the pass mark",null,"Divided 105 by 30%"],"calc":"(98+7)/0.35"},
  {"id":"pct-marks-04","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"exam-marks","d":"Easy","q":"A student scored **65%** in Maths (out of **200**) and **80%** in Science (out of **100**). What is the total marks scored?","opts":["145","300","130","210"],"ans":3,"time":45,"sol":["Maths = 65% of 200 = 130","Science = 80% of 100 = 80; total = **210**"],"short":"Percent of 200 is double the percent figure: 130; plus 80.","trap":"Percentages cannot be added across papers of different totals.","why":["Added 65 + 80","Gave the maximum total","Counted only Maths",null],"calc":"0.65*200+0.8*100"},
  {"id":"pct-marks-05","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"exam-marks","d":"Easy","q":"Rita scores **80%** in a paper of **150** marks. Sam scores **90** marks. How many more marks did Rita score than Sam?","opts":["20","30","120","10"],"ans":1,"time":40,"sol":["Rita = 80% of 150 = 120","Rita − Sam = 120 − 90 = **30**"],"short":"Sam has 60%, so Rita is 20% of 150 ahead = 30.","trap":"Convert both to marks before subtracting.","why":["Gave the difference in percentages",null,"Gave Rita's marks","Subtracted 80 − 70"],"calc":"0.8*150-90"},
  {"id":"pct-marks-06","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"exam-marks","d":"Medium","q":"A student needs **40%** to pass. He scored **180** and failed by **20** marks. What are the maximum marks?","opts":["450","1,000","500","400"],"ans":2,"time":65,"sol":["Pass mark = 180 + 20 = 200","40% of max = 200, so max = 200 ÷ 0.4 = **500**"],"short":"200 is 40%, so 10% = 50 and 100% = 500.","trap":"Failing by 20 means the pass mark is 20 above his score.","why":["Ignored the failing margin","Divided the pass mark by 20%",null,"Took 200 as 50% of the paper"],"calc":"(180+20)/0.4"},
  {"id":"pct-marks-07","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"exam-marks","d":"Medium","q":"In a paper, Asha scored **55%** and Bala scored **40%**. Asha got **60** more marks than Bala. What are the maximum marks?","opts":["600","240","150","400"],"ans":3,"time":60,"sol":["Difference in percent = 55 − 40 = 15%","15% of max = 60, so max = **400**"],"short":"15% = 60, so 5% = 20 and 100% = 400.","trap":"Use the difference of the two percentages, not either of them.","why":["Divided by a 10% gap","Divided by 25%","Divided by 40%",null],"calc":"60/0.15"},
  {"id":"pct-marks-08","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"exam-marks","d":"Medium","q":"A student scored **75%** in Physics (out of 80), **60%** in Chemistry (out of 100) and **90%** in Maths (out of 120). What is the overall percentage?","opts":["76%","75%","90%","228%"],"ans":0,"time":75,"sol":["Marks: 60 + 60 + 108 = 228","Maximum = 300, so 228 ÷ 300 = **76%**"],"short":"Total marks over total maximum, never the mean of percentages.","trap":"Papers of different totals need weighting.","why":[null,"Averaged the three percentages","Took the best subject","Gave marks scored as a percentage"],"calc":"(0.75*80+0.6*100+0.9*120)/300*100"},
  {"id":"pct-marks-09","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"exam-marks","d":"Medium","q":"One student gets **30%** of the marks and fails by **30** marks. Another gets **40%** and passes by **20** marks. What is the pass mark?","opts":["500","220","180","150"],"ans":2,"time":75,"sol":["Difference of the two scores = 10% of max = 30 + 20 = 50, so max = 500","Pass mark = 30% of 500 + 30 = **180**"],"short":"The gap between the two students (50 marks) equals 10% of the paper.","trap":"Failing adds to the score, passing subtracts from it, to reach the pass mark.","why":["Gave the maximum marks","Added the passing margin to 40%",null,"Took 30% of the maximum"],"calc":"0.3*500+30"},
  {"id":"pct-marks-10","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"exam-marks","d":"Medium","q":"To pass, a student needs **60%** of the total of three papers of **100** marks each. Anil scored **55** and **62** in the first two. What must he score in the third to just pass?","opts":["60","117","120","63"],"ans":3,"time":70,"sol":["Total needed = 60% of 300 = 180","Third paper = 180 − (55 + 62) = **63**"],"short":"Needs 180; has 117; 180 − 117.","trap":"The 60% is on the aggregate, not on every paper.","why":["Needed 60 in each paper","Gave the marks already scored","Subtracted only 60",null],"calc":"180-55-62"},
  {"id":"pct-marks-11","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"exam-marks","d":"Medium","q":"**2,000** students registered for an exam; **90%** appeared and **70%** of those who appeared passed. How many of those who appeared failed?","opts":["540","600","1,260","740"],"ans":0,"time":60,"sol":["Appeared = 90% of 2,000 = 1,800","Failed = 30% of 1,800 = **540**"],"short":"0.9 × 0.3 × 2,000 = 540.","trap":"The 70% is of those who appeared, not of those who registered.","why":[null,"Took 30% of all 2,000","Gave the number who passed","Subtracted passers from 2,000"],"calc":"2000*0.9*0.3"},
  {"id":"pct-marks-12","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"exam-marks","d":"Hard","q":"In a paper of **800** marks, Z scored **75%**. Y scored **36% less** than Z and X scored **25% more** than Y. How many marks did X score?","opts":["384","480","600","750"],"ans":1,"time":100,"sol":["Z = 75% of 800 = 600; Y = 0.64 × 600 = 384","X = 1.25 × 384 = **480**"],"short":"0.64 × 1.25 = 0.8, so X = 80% of Z = 480.","trap":"Each comparison is with a different person, so chain them in order.","why":["Stopped at Y's marks",null,"Gave Z's marks","Added 25% to Z directly"],"calc":"800*0.75*0.64*1.25"},
  {"id":"pct-marks-13","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"exam-marks","d":"Hard","q":"The pass mark is **36%**. A student who scores **25%** fails by **44** marks. Another student scores **45%**. By how many marks does he pass?","opts":["44","180","144","36"],"ans":3,"time":100,"sol":["Gap 36% − 25% = 11% of max = 44, so max = 400","Pass mark = 144; his marks = 180; he passes by 180 − 144 = **36**"],"short":"Margin = (45 − 36)% of max = 9% of 400.","trap":"Both gaps are measured from the same pass mark, so 9% of max.","why":["Reused the failing margin","Gave his marks","Gave the pass mark",null],"calc":"0.09*44/0.11"},
  {"id":"pct-marks-14","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"exam-marks","d":"Hard","q":"Aman needs **35%** of the total to pass. Paper I: he scores **40%** of **150**; Paper II: **30%** of **250**. Paper III is out of **200**. What percentage must he score in Paper III to just pass?","opts":["37.5%","35%","40%","30%"],"ans":0,"time":100,"sol":["Total maximum = 600, needed = 35% of 600 = 210; scored = 60 + 75 = 135","Needed in III = 75 ÷ 200 = **37.5%**"],"short":"Needed 210 − 135 = 75 marks out of 200.","trap":"The aggregate target is in marks, not an average of the papers' percentages.","why":[null,"Gave the overall target","Copied Paper I's percentage","Copied Paper II's percentage"],"calc":"(0.35*600-0.4*150-0.3*250)/200*100"},
  {"id":"pct-marks-15","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"exam-marks","d":"Hard","q":"In a class, **60%** of the students are boys. **70%** of the boys and **50%** of the girls pass. If **190** students failed, how many students are in the class?","opts":["475","500","380","≈ 306"],"ans":1,"time":100,"sol":["Fail rate = 0.6 × 30% + 0.4 × 50% = 18% + 20% = 38%","Class = 190 ÷ 0.38 = **500**"],"short":"Take 100 students: 18 boys + 20 girls fail = 38, so scale to 190.","trap":"Weight the fail rates by group size.","why":["Divided by 40% (the girls' share)",null,"Divided by 50%","Divided by 62%, the pass rate"],"calc":"190/0.38"},
  {"id":"pct-marks-16","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"exam-marks","d":"Hard","q":"The pass mark is **35%** of the maximum. Sachin got **20% less** than the pass mark and failed by **21** marks. What are the maximum marks?","opts":["60","525","300","375"],"ans":2,"time":110,"sol":["20% of the pass mark = 21, so pass mark = 105","35% of max = 105, so max = **300**"],"short":"21 is a fifth of the pass mark; pass mark = 105 = 35% of 300.","trap":"The 20% is of the pass mark, not of the maximum.","why":["Divided 21 by 35%","Treated 105 as 20% of the maximum",null,"Divided 105 by 28%"],"calc":"21/0.2/0.35"},
  {"id":"pct-back-01","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"back-calc","d":"Easy","q":"After a **20%** raise, Karan's salary is **₹36,000**. What was it before the raise?","opts":["₹30,000","₹28,800","₹43,200","₹7,200"],"ans":0,"time":35,"sol":["New = old × 1.20","Old = 36,000 ÷ 1.2 = **₹30,000**"],"short":"Divide by 1.2: 36 ÷ 1.2 = 30.","trap":"The 20% is of the old salary, so never take 20% off the new one.","why":[null,"Took 20% off the new salary","Added 20% to the new salary","Gave the raise amount"],"calc":"36000/1.2"},
  {"id":"pct-back-02","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"back-calc","d":"Easy","q":"After **25%** of a sack of rice is sold, **45 kg** remain. How much rice was there originally?","opts":["56.25 kg","60 kg","33.75 kg","70 kg"],"ans":1,"time":30,"sol":["Remaining = 75% of the original","Original = 45 ÷ 0.75 = **60 kg**"],"short":"45 is 3/4, so one quarter is 15 and the whole is 60.","trap":"Undo a decrease by dividing by 0.75.","why":["Multiplied by 1.25",null,"Took 75% of the remainder","Added 25 to 45"],"calc":"45/0.75"},
  {"id":"pct-back-03","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"back-calc","d":"Easy","q":"A shirt is sold at **10% off** the marked price for **₹1,260**. What is the marked price?","opts":["₹1,386","₹1,134","₹1,400","₹1,270"],"ans":2,"time":35,"sol":["Sale price = 90% of marked price","Marked = 1,260 ÷ 0.9 = **₹1,400**"],"short":"90% = 1,260, so 10% = 140 and 100% = 1,400.","trap":"The discount is on the marked price, so divide by 0.9.","why":["Added 10% to the sale price","Took 10% off the sale price",null,"Added a flat ₹10"],"calc":"1260/0.9"},
  {"id":"pct-back-04","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"back-calc","d":"Easy","q":"A bag of sugar became **20% lighter** after spillage and now weighs **8 kg**. What was its original weight?","opts":["9.6 kg","6.4 kg","2 kg","10 kg"],"ans":3,"time":35,"sol":["8 kg is 80% of the original","Original = 8 ÷ 0.8 = **10 kg**"],"short":"80% = 8, so 10% = 1 and 100% = 10.","trap":"Go from the 80% that is left back to 100%.","why":["Added 20% to 8 kg","Took 20% off 8 kg","Gave the amount spilled",null],"calc":"8/0.8"},
  {"id":"pct-back-05","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"back-calc","d":"Easy","q":"The price of petrol rose **8%** and is now **₹108** per litre. What was the price before the rise?","opts":["₹116.64","₹100","₹99.36","₹97.2"],"ans":1,"time":40,"sol":["New price = old × 1.08","Old = 108 ÷ 1.08 = **₹100**"],"short":"108 = 1.08 × 100, so the old price is 100.","trap":"The 8% was on the old price; use division, not subtraction.","why":["Added 8% to ₹108",null,"Took 8% off ₹108","Took 10% off ₹108"],"calc":"108/1.08"},
  {"id":"pct-back-06","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"back-calc","d":"Medium","q":"A phone gets a **15% discount** on its marked price, then **10% GST** is added on the discounted price. The final bill is **₹18,700**. What is the marked price?","opts":["≈ ₹19,684","₹17,000","₹20,000","₹14,450"],"ans":2,"time":75,"sol":["Bill = marked × 0.85 × 1.10 = marked × 0.935","Marked = 18,700 ÷ 0.935 = **₹20,000**"],"short":"18,700 ÷ 1.1 = 17,000; 17,000 ÷ 0.85 = 20,000.","trap":"Reverse each step with the opposite operation, in reverse order.","why":["Treated it as a net 5% discount","Undid only the GST",null,"Undid the GST, then took off the discount again"],"calc":"18700/(0.85*1.1)"},
  {"id":"pct-back-07","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"back-calc","d":"Medium","q":"A grocer sold **20%** of his mangoes in the morning and **25%** of the rest in the evening. **120** mangoes were left. How many did he start with?","opts":["≈ 218","174","150","200"],"ans":3,"time":65,"sol":["Left = original × 0.80 × 0.75 = 0.6 × original","Original = 120 ÷ 0.6 = **200**"],"short":"0.8 × 0.75 = 0.6 = 3/5, so the original is 120 × 5/3.","trap":"20% and 25% are on different bases and cannot be added.","why":["Treated it as 45% sold","Added 45% back","Undid only the evening sale as +25%",null],"calc":"120/(0.8*0.75)"},
  {"id":"pct-back-08","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"back-calc","d":"Medium","q":"Ramesh's salary was cut by **10%** and then raised by **20%**. It is now **₹43,200**. What was it originally?","opts":["₹40,000","≈ ₹39,273","₹48,000","₹36,000"],"ans":0,"time":65,"sol":["Net factor = 0.90 × 1.20 = 1.08","Original = 43,200 ÷ 1.08 = **₹40,000**"],"short":"Net = −10 + 20 − 2 = +8%, so divide by 1.08.","trap":"Net of −10% and +20% is +8%, not +10%.","why":[null,"Treated it as a net +10%","Undid only the cut","Undid only the raise"],"calc":"43200/(0.9*1.2)"},
  {"id":"pct-back-09","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"back-calc","d":"Medium","q":"A tank lost **30%** of its water on day 1 and **20%** of what was left on day 2. **5,600 litres** remain. How much water was there at first?","opts":["11,200 L","7,000 L","10,000 L","8,400 L"],"ans":2,"time":70,"sol":["Remaining fraction = 0.70 × 0.80 = 0.56","Start = 5,600 ÷ 0.56 = **10,000 L**"],"short":"56% is 5,600, so 1% is 100 and 100% is 10,000.","trap":"Multiply the remaining fractions, not the losses.","why":["Treated it as 50% left","Undid only day 2",null,"Added 50% back"],"calc":"5600/(0.7*0.8)"},
  {"id":"pct-back-10","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"back-calc","d":"Medium","q":"A laptop's price rose **25%** after one season and then fell **20%** the next. It now costs **₹60,000**. What was the original price?","opts":["₹75,000","₹48,000","₹62,500","₹60,000"],"ans":3,"time":70,"sol":["Net factor = 1.25 × 0.80 = 1.00","No net change, so the original was **₹60,000**"],"short":"+25% then −20% cancel exactly: 5/4 × 4/5 = 1.","trap":"Always check whether the factors multiply to 1 before dividing.","why":["Multiplied by 1.25","Multiplied by 0.8","Treated it as a net −4%",null],"calc":"60000/(1.25*0.8)"},
  {"id":"pct-back-11","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"back-calc","d":"Medium","q":"A student's score rose **20%** from Test 1 to Test 2, then fell **10%** to Test 3, where it was **81**. What was the Test 1 score?","opts":["75","90","≈ 73.6","97.2"],"ans":0,"time":70,"sol":["Net factor = 1.20 × 0.90 = 1.08","Test 1 = 81 ÷ 1.08 = **75**"],"short":"81 ÷ 1.08 = 75 (1.08 × 75 = 81).","trap":"Net +8%, not +10%: the 10% fall is on the bigger score.","why":[null,"Undid only the fall","Treated it as a net +10%","Multiplied by 1.2"],"calc":"81/(1.2*0.9)"},
  {"id":"pct-back-12","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"back-calc","d":"Hard","q":"A number is decreased by **20%**, the result is increased by **25%**, and that result is decreased by **10%**, giving **180**. What is the number?","opts":["≈ 189.5","200","225","162"],"ans":1,"time":90,"sol":["Net factor = 0.80 × 1.25 × 0.90 = 0.90","Number = 180 ÷ 0.90 = **200**"],"short":"0.8 × 1.25 = 1, so only the last 10% matters.","trap":"Look for steps that cancel before doing any division.","why":["Treated it as a net −5%",null,"Undid only the first step","Multiplied by 0.9"],"calc":"180/(0.8*1.25*0.9)"},
  {"id":"pct-back-13","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"back-calc","d":"Hard","q":"A bill shows a **10%** discount on the marked price and then **12% GST** on the discounted price. The GST charged is **₹1,080**. What is the marked price?","opts":["₹9,000","₹10,080","₹10,800","₹10,000"],"ans":3,"time":90,"sol":["GST = 12% of the discounted price = 1,080, so discounted = 9,000","Marked = 9,000 ÷ 0.9 = **₹10,000**"],"short":"1,080 → 9,000 → 10,000: ÷0.12 then ÷0.9.","trap":"GST is on the discounted price, not on the marked price.","why":["Gave the discounted price","Added the GST to the discounted price","Divided the GST by 10%",null],"calc":"1080/0.12/0.9"},
  {"id":"pct-back-14","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"back-calc","d":"Hard","q":"Meera gave away **25%** of her chocolates, ate **20%** of the rest, then gave **one-third** of what remained to her brother. She has **24** left. How many did she start with?","opts":["60","36","72","≈ 110.8"],"ans":0,"time":100,"sol":["Remaining fraction = 0.75 × 0.80 × 2/3 = 0.4","Start = 24 ÷ 0.4 = **60**"],"short":"24 → 36 → 45 → 60: ×3/2, ×5/4, ×4/3.","trap":"Giving away a third leaves two-thirds.","why":[null,"Undid only the last step","Divided by 1/3 instead of 2/3","Subtracted 25%, 20% and 33⅓% together"],"calc":"24/(0.75*0.8*2/3)"},
  {"id":"pct-back-15","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"back-calc","d":"Hard","q":"A factory's output rose **20%** in 2022, fell in 2023, and rose **25%** in 2024, ending exactly where it began in 2021. By what percentage did output fall in 2023?","opts":["45%","33⅓%","20%","66⅔%"],"ans":1,"time":100,"sol":["1.20 × (1 − x) × 1.25 = 1, so 1 − x = 1/1.5 = 2/3","Fall x = **33⅓%**"],"short":"The rises give 1.5×, so output must drop to 2/3, a fall of one-third.","trap":"A fall must be measured from the higher base, so it is smaller than the combined rise.","why":["Added the rises 20 + 25",null,"Mirrored the first rise","Gave the fraction remaining"],"calc":"100/3"},
  {"id":"pct-back-16","sec":"quant","topic":"percentages","pattern":"back-calc","d":"Hard","q":"Anita spent **30%** of her money on books, then **25% of the remainder** on clothes, and then **₹630** on food. She has **₹420** left. How much money did she have at the start?","opts":["₹1,500","₹1,400","₹2,000","₹2,100"],"ans":2,"time":110,"sol":["Before food she had 420 + 630 = 1,050","That is 0.70 × 0.75 = 0.525 of the start, so start = 1,050 ÷ 0.525 = **₹2,000**"],"short":"1,050 ÷ 0.75 = 1,400; 1,400 ÷ 0.70 = 2,000.","trap":"Add the food spending back first; it is a fixed amount, not a percentage.","why":["Divided by 70% only","Divided by 75% only",null,"Divided by 50%"],"calc":"(420+630)/(0.7*0.75)"}
]);
